★ Insight ─────────────────────────────────────
Maven's `provided` scope is intentionally non-transitive — it signals "the consumer already has this on their classpath some other way" (an app server, an uber-jar, another artifact). Gradle respects that scope boundary when resolving POM metadata, so artifacts like `rt-fi` that bridge two libraries (`rt` ↔ `FastInfoset`) commonly need the "API-side" dependency re-declared explicitly by downstream consumers, even though the bridge module can't function without it at compile time.
